# Bifunctional iron-phtalocyanine metal–organic framework catalyst for ORR, OER and rechargeable zinc–air battery

Abstract Exploring non-noble metal and high-activity electrocatalysts through a simple and controllable protocol remains a great challenge for oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) and zinc–air batteries. Herein, we developed a melt polymerization strategy to synthesize iron-polyphthalocyanine (FePPc) metallic–organic frameworks (MOFs) over the carbon black matrix $({\mathrm{FePPc}}@{\mathbf{CB}})$ . Through noncovalent $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions, FePPc molecules can anchor on carbon matrix, thus facilitating the electron transfer process and stabilizing the systems. Owing to abundant free electrons and atomically $\mathrm{MN}_{4}$ catalytic sites in the macrocycle structure, $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ exhibits excellent oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) electrocatalytic activity. The $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ also delivers excellent performances for liquid and flexible all-solidstate batteries compared to that of commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ , making it a promising ORR/OER electrocatalyst.
Keywords Metal–organic frameworks; Poly-ironpolyphthalocyanine; Oxygen reduction reaction; Zn–air battery
## 1 Introduction
One key factor to prepare new types of energy storage and conversion system such as metal–air battery and fuel cell is electrochemical reaction, including oxygen reduction/evolution reaction (ORR/OER) [1–6]. However, their sluggish kinetics and low activities still remain a challenge. Traditional noble-metal platinum $\mathrm{(Pt)}$ and $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ could act as an efficient catalyst for ORR and OER, respectively. But its scarcity, such as high cost and poor durability, has hindered the widespread application of $\mathrm{Pt}$ -based catalysts [7–11]. So far, numerous efforts and advances have been made to find convenient and available candidates. For example, metal/ nitrogen/carbon (M/N/C, M, metal $=\mathrm{Co}$ , Fe, Ni) catalysts, which are recognized as the most promising candidate, could perform high activities and excellent stability with low cost [12–17]. While the introduction of other catalytic and composites active sites are inevitable in elevated temperature exposure, it is still a great challenge to eluidate the origin of catalytic center in M/N/C electrocatalysts [18].
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are composed of central metal ions and ligands and possess readily tunable structural chemistry and the selectable metal ions as distributed active sites [19–27]. Recent studies have proved that MOFs could bring desirable enhancement in catalytic performance recently as a kind of promising precursors during the synthetic process of non-noble-metal catalysts. Fe-based compounds have been broadly investigated among the high-valent metal ions. Moreover, researches about Fe-based MOFs as electrocatalyst have been widely discussed recently, which revealed that Fe-based MOFs could facilitate oxygen involving reaction [28]. However, it is still a challenge to search the higher activity for the MOF catalyst that can suppress the benchmark $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ . On the other hand, metal macrocycles were firstly demonstrated to promote ORR cell in 1960s, and then triggered extensive research as promising cathode catalyst in fuel cell and metal–air battery [29]. The well-identified $\mathrm{MN}_{4}$ structure in metal macrocycles, such as metal phtalocyanines (MPc) and metal porphyrins (MP), has been proved facilitated to catalyze the electrochemical ORR [30–32]. Numerous metal macrocycles-based catalysts have been explored as demonstrated by the FePc groups grafting on single-walled CNTs [31], self-assembly FePc molecule over the CNTs [32], and FePc molecule anchoring on N-doped carbon [18]. Particularly for FePc molecule-based electrocatalysts suffering multiplex synthesis and poor stability, the real structure could hardly be strictly controlled, thus hindering the further exploration of experimental results and practical cell application.
Herein, we put forward a novel catalyst of ironpolyphthalocyanine metallic–organic frameworks (MOFs) over carbon black matrix $({\mathrm{FePPc}}@\mathbf{CB})$ via melt polymerization process. Through strong non-covalent $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions, FePPc molecular can adsorb on carbon matrix, which would facilitate the electron transfer process and make the systems stable. In addition, lots of free electrons saunter over the macrocycle system, and make the catalytic reaction easier to take place. Moreover, there are much more abundant highly dispersed $\mathrm{Fe-N_{4}}$ catalytic active sites in FePPc MOF structure than in single iron-polyphthalocyanine adsorbed on $({\mathrm{FePc}}@C\mathbf{B})$ . Therefore, the as-prepared catalyst exhibits excellent bifunctional electrocatalytic performances for ORR and OER, including high activity, excellent durability and good tolerance to methanol. Therefore, liquid and flexible all-solid-state rechargeable zinc–air batteries (ZABs) were also evaluated and showed excellent performance.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Synthesis of FePPc $@$ CB catalyst
In a typical synthesis of $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ , $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ $(0.500\ \mathrm{mmol})$ , urea $\left(0.035~\mathrm{mol}\right)$ , $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{4}C l}$ $\left(0.009\mathrm{mol}\right)$ , $(\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}$ $(0.037\ \mathrm{mmol})$ , pyromellitic dianhydride $(0.005~\mathrm{mol})$ , and $25~\mathrm{mg}$ carbon black matrix were fully blended and then milled uniformly. Then, the above mixture was moved to a crucible. After heated to $220^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ in a muffle furnace with a ramp rate of $2\mathrm{\^{\circ}C}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , the as-obtained products were separated via centrifugation and washed with water, acetone and methyl alcohol, respectively, and dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a vacuum oven overnight. For contrast, the synthesis of pure-FePPc is almost the same as that of $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ except the adding of carbon matrix.
## 2.2 Synthesis of FePc@CB catalyst
Typically, $5~\mathrm{mg}$ iron phthalocyanine (FePc) was dissolved in $50~\mathrm{ml}$ ethanol, followed by adding $25{\mathrm{~mg~CB}}$ to the above solution. After strong sonication for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ , the mixed solution was further stirred at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The ethanol would gradually evaporate and finally a black powder was obtained. The as-prepared sample was marked as FePc@CB [18].
## 2.3 Characterizations
The morphology of the samples was studied by fieldemission scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM, JEORJSM-6700F). The transmission electron microscope (TEM, FEI Tecnai G2 20) was performed with an accelerating voltage of $200\ensuremath{\mathrm{\kV}}$ . The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements were performed with an ESCA LAB 250 spectrometer on a focused monochromatic Al $\mathbf{\Delta}\mathsf{K}\mathsf{\bgroup{a}}$ line $(1486.6\ \mathrm{eV})$ X-ray beam with a diameter of $200~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) was performed on a Bruker Vertex 70v FTIR spectrometer. The M¨ossbauer measurements were performed using a conventional spectrometer (Germany, Wissel MS-500) in transmission geometry with constant acceleration mode. A 57Co(Rh) source with activity of $25~\mathrm{{mCi}}$ was used. The velocity calibration was done with a room temperature $\textsf{a}$ - Fe absorber. The spectra were fitted by the software Recoil using Lorentzian Site Analysis.
## 2.4 Electrochemical measurements
All electrochemical measurements were conducted with a three-electrode system on CHI760E electrochemical workstation (CH Instrument Co., USA). The $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ (saturated KCl aqueous solution) electrode was used as the reference electrode and $\mathrm{\sfPt}$ foil was used as the counter electrode with a standard electrochemical cell (Pine Instrument). All the tests were proceeded in $0.1\ \mathrm{mol{\cdot}L^{-1}}$ KOH electrolyte at room temperature $(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . Before the electrochemical measurements, the electrolyte solution was purged with $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ to achieve the $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ -saturated solution, and stable polarization curves were recorded after 20 cycles. Reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) calibration was performed in high-purity hydrogen-saturated electrolyte with a $\mathrm{\sfPt}$ foil as the working electrode and a Pt wire as the counter electrode. The cyclic voltammetry (CV) experiments were cycled in $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ -saturated $0.1\ \mathrm{mol{\cdot}L^{-1}}$ KOH electrolyte solutions with a scan rate of $50\ \mathrm{mV}\cdot\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . The potentials used in this work were normalized to a RHE according to the Nernst equation $(E_{\mathrm{RHE}}=E_{\mathrm{Ag/AgC1}}+$ 0.964). Typically, the working electrode was prepared as follows: $2~\mathrm{mg}$ catalyst and $1~\mathrm{mm}$ ethanol with $5\%$ nafion
