## 3 Results and discussion
To prepare $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ composite materials, the reaction between pyromellitic dianhydride and $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ was carried out at $220^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , in which the reactants were all in molten state. Pyromellitic dianhydride will polymerize into macromolecule and Fe atoms would insert into the center of phthalocyanine molecules. CB is of good conductivity for the electron transport and abundant free electrons saunter over the FePPc structure. The synthetic process is illustrated in Scheme 1. The melt polymerization method here is convenient and highly reproducible. Moreover, a gram-scale production can be promising.
As shown in Fig. 1a, two broad peaks at about $25^{\circ}$ and $44^{\circ}$ are observed in XRD patterns of the as-prepared catalysts, which is assigned to (002) and (101) planes of carbon, respectively, indicating that no Fe or $\mathrm{FeO}_{x}$ particles formed. Field-emission scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and TEM images of $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ present amorphous morphology with abundant porous structure (Fig. 1b, c). FTIR patterns of $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ in comparison with pure FePc and $\mathrm{FePPc}$ are shown in Fig. 1e. The pure FePc exhibits characteristic bands from $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}-\mathbf{C}$ stretching (1611, 1421 and $1335~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1},}$ ), $\mathrm{C-H}$ bending (1514, 1466, 1121, 1086, 907, 754 and $721~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ and $\mathrm{C-N}$ stretching (1288 and $1167~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ). Compared to FePc, the bands at 1767 and $1711~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ in both FePPc and $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ are associated with $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ stretching from the side reactions of nitrile groups during the polymerization. In addition, their $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C-C}$ stretching bands (1570, 636 and $527~\mathrm{cm}^{-1},$ ), C–H bending (903, 800, 744 and $725~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) and the frame vibrations of the incorporated aromatic benzene and pyrrole rings (from 1616 to $1026~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}},$ ) all confirm the formation of FePPc polymer [33]. Figure 1d presents a model of FePPc. Energy-dispersive $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mapping (Fig. 1f) indicates that C, N and Fe are distributed uniformly over the entire architecture, proving the strong interaction between FePPc and carbon matrix. The above results proved that FePPc polymerizes around CB matrix homogenously and form quite a stable system.
XPS was performed to analyze the detailed elemental composition and states of the FePPc@CB [34]. As shown in Fig. 2a, XPS survey spectrum confirms the existence of Fe, C, N and O, with the respective atom ratio of $0.22\%$ , $78.41\%$ , $8.82\%$ and $12.55\%$ , respectively (Table 1). The N 1s spectrum of $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ (Fig. 2b) indicates three types of $\mathbf{N}$ species: pyrrolic $(399.96{\mathrm{~eV}})$ , pyridinic $(398.21\ \mathrm{eV})$ and Fe–N species $(398.18\ \mathrm{eV})$ [35]. The two distinct Fe $2\ensuremath{\mathrm{p}}_{1/2}$ and $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ peaks located at 720.49 and $710.77\ \mathrm{eV}$ with shakeup satellites (denoted as ‘‘Sat.’’) at 724.03 and $712.93\ \mathrm{eV}$ in XPS Fe 2p results in Fig. 2c are considered as the high-valence-state Fe atom, namely $\mathrm{Fe\mathrm{-}N}$ species [36, 37]. Moreover, the chemical properties of Fe are well retained after durable test, which proves that $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ is quite stable. The $^{57}\mathrm{Fe}$ Mo¨ssbauer spectroscopy was also performed to make a deep insight into the Fe species. As shown in Fig. 2d, three components was fitted: Doublet 1 (D1) is commonly attributed to FePc-like $\mathrm{Fe^{II}N_{4}}$ species; Doublet 2 (D2) can be attributed to ${\bf X}{\mathrm{-Fe}}^{\mathrm{III}}\mathrm{N}_{4}{\mathrm{-Y}}$ high-spin species (X and $\mathrm{Y}$ refer to $\mathrm{~o~}$ or $\mathbf{N}$ ligand), where the Fe atoms are drew out of the $\mathrm{N}_{4}$ plane; Sextet 1 can be assigned to $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ which belongs to $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ [38, 39]. As we know, Fe atoms in FePc molecules are deemed as middle spin state. While the electronic structure of the central metal atoms is obviously affected by the MOF structure and boosted to high-spin state. According to further quantitative analysis, the contents of D1, D2 and Sextet 1 species were $13.2\%$ , $45.7\%$ and $41.1\%$ , respectively, confirming that most Fe species in $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ are high-spin stat, which could boost the catalytic activity in ORR process [40].
The ORR catalytic activity of $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ and $\mathrm{FePc}@\mathbf{CB}$ are assessed in a three-electrode cell using $\mathbf{O}_{2}.$ - saturated $0.1\ \mathrm{mol{\cdot}L^{-1}\ K O F}$ solution as the electrolyte, and compared with benchmark commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalysts. As shown in Fig. 3a, pure carbon matrix shows almost no activity for ORR. Among the four catalysts, $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ displays a distinctly more positive activity with a half-wave potential $(E_{1/2})$ of $0.908{\mathrm{~V~}}$ versus RHE, 59 and $77~\mathrm{mV}$ higher, respectively, than those of $\operatorname{FePc}@\mathbf{CB}$ and $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalysts. Moreover, $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ reaches a limiting current density of $5.38\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ as high as $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ . Compared with $\mathrm{FePc}@\mathbf{CB}$ , the $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ catalyst possess much more active sites, thus promoting the ORR catalytic activity. The low Tafel slope at low overpotential (Fig. 3b) of our catalyst is only $29\mathrm{\mV{\cdot}d e c^{-1}}$ , lower than that of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ $(77\ \mathrm{mV\cdotdec}^{-1},$ and $\mathrm{FePc}@\mathbf{CB}$ ${75\ \mathrm{mV\cdotdec}^{-1}};$ , demonstrating the fast kinetics for ORR. Specifically, the $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ sample exhibits high kinetic current density $(J_{\mathrm{k}})$ of $7.44~\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $0.5\mathrm{~V~}$ (Fig. 3c) in alkaline solutions. Compared with $\mathrm{FePc}@\mathbf{CB}$ , $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ possesses a large density of active sites, namely $\mathrm{FeN}_{4}$ center. Moreover, abundant free electrons hang over in the macrocycle structure, thus promoting ORR catalytic process. It is known that the ORR process is desirable to proceed according to four-electron transfer. However, two-electron reaction which produces $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ could take place as a side reaction. To investigate the electron-transfer mechanism, rotating ring-disk electrode (RRDE) measurements were carried out (Fig. 3d). The $n$ value of $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ is 3.93–4.01 and the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ yield is below $3.5\%$ within $0.2\mathrm{-}0.8\mathrm{~V~}$ .
Chronoamperometric response at $0.75{\mathrm{~V~}}$ (vs. RHE) was performed to evaluate the durability of $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ . As shown in Fig. 3e, the current density remains $73\%$ after 40,000 s. Inset in Fig. 3e is CV curves before and after $i-$ $t$ test. It is obvious that the reduction peak shows almost no shift. A key factor for excellent catalyst is against fuel poisoning during ORR process, in particular the tolerance to methanol. Unlike the Pt-based materials, this $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ catalyst shows tolerance to methanol with a remained current density of $90\%$ after 2000 s (Fig. 3f).
The variance of OER and ORR metrics was deemed as the index for bifunctional catalyst of ORR and OER, in which $\Delta E=E_{j=10}-E_{1/2}$ $(E_{\mathrm{j=10}}$ is the OER potential taken at a current density of $10\ \mathrm{m}\mathrm{\bar{A}}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ ). As shown in Fig. 4a, the $\Delta E$ value for $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ is $0.68\mathrm{~V~}$ , obviously superior to that of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ (for ORR) and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ (for OER). The excellent ORR/OER bifunctional activity of $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ encourages us to explore its further application as cathode catalyst for alkaline Zn–air batteries.
According to the model in Fig. 4b, we constructed a liquid $Z\mathrm{n}$ –air battery [2, 41–43]. As anticipated, the liquid rechargeable ZAB delivered a high open-circuit voltage $(\approx1.4~\mathrm{V})$ and a large power density of $\approx90\mathrm{\mW}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ with a corresponding current density of $\approx240\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (Fig. 4c, d), both of which are much better than those of ZABs with $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalysts. The stability and reversibility are equally important for ZABs. As shown in Fig. 4e, the galvanostatic discharge–charge cycling test operated about $60\mathrm{~h~}$ at $5\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ with a scan rate of 400 s per cycle. Furthermore, a red light (light emitting diode, LED, $8~\mathrm{mm}$ , $\approx2.2\:\mathrm{V}$ ) could be powered by two liquid ZABs in series (Fig. 4f), which possess $1.367\mathrm{~V~}$ high open-circuit voltage. In long-term discharge test at $10.0\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ in Fig. $4\mathrm{g}$ , the potential curve stays stable and presents no obvious decrease. Combining with the mass of consumed zinc foil, the specific capacity could be calculated as $1391\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ , corresponding to a high energy density of $\approx1317~\mathrm{{Wh}{\cdot k g}^{-1}}$ . As shown in Fig. 4h, the excellent rate capability can be revealed by the voltage drop at different discharge current densities from 2 to $20\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ .
In addition, we assembled all-solid-state and flexible allsolid-state ZABs, both of which comprise of a negative zinc electrode, PVP gel, Ni foam and a positive air electrode assembled together in an alkaline electrolyte. For allsolid-state ZABs, galvanostatic discharge–charge cycling curves at $2\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (Fig. 5a) demonstrate the high stability. As shown in Fig. 5b, the battery could reach an open-circuit as high as $1.321\mathrm{~V~}$ and a green LED 1 $(\approx3.0\:\mathrm{V})$ could be powered. For flexible all-solid-state ZABs, when bended to various angle $(0^{\circ},90^{\circ},145^{\circ}$ and $180^{\circ})$ , stable charge $(1.5{\mathrm{~V}})$ and discharge $(1.0\mathrm{V})$ potentials at the current density of $2\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ (Fig. 5c) could be held. Moreover, the open-circuit voltages suffer little about the bending angle and stay quit similar. The results of ORR, OER, liquid and solid reversible $\mathtt{Z n\mathrm{-Air}}$ battery demonstrate the excellent electrocatalytic performance of $\mathrm{FePPc/CB}$ hybrid electrocatalysts.
## 4 Conclusion
In conclusion, we have developed a melt polymerization method to synthesize iron-polyphthalocyanine metallic– organic frameworks (MOFs) sheathed around carbon black matrix $({\mathrm{FePPc}}@{\mathrm{CB}})$ . Through non-covalent $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions, FePPc molecules can adsorb on carbon matrix, thus facilitating the electron transfer process and stabilizing the systems. Owing to abundant free electrons and atomically $\mathrm{MN}_{4}$ catalytic sites in the macrocycle structure, $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ shows superior ORR activity with $\boldsymbol{E}_{1/2}$ $=0.908\mathrm{~V~}$ and $j_{\mathrm{L}}=5.38\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . In addition, the $\Delta E$ value of $0.68\mathrm{~V~}$ for ORR and OER makes the $\operatorname{FePPc}@\mathbf{CB}$ catalyst a promising candidate for liquid and solid-state rechargeable $Z\mathrm{n}$ –air batteries (ZABs). For liquid ZABs, an open-circuit potential of $\mathrm{~\sim~}1.4\mathrm{~V~}$ , a peak power density of $\sim90\mathrm{\mW}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , as well as excellent stability (for $60\mathrm{~h~}$ of operation) could be achieved. It is worth emphasizing the unique advantages in the ease and scalability for the preparation of our $\mathrm{FePPc/CB}$ hybrid electrocatalysts in absence of energy intensive high-temperature polyposis. Given the various structures of MOF, the novel type of composite electrocatalyst is of large potential for energy storage and conversion systems and deserves much further instigation.
